The following Python class is a simple implementation of a Stack data structure. It provides methods to push items onto the stack, pop items off the stack, and check if the stack is empty.

```python
class Stack:
    def __init__(self):
        self._items = []

    def push(self, item):
        self._items.append(item)

    def pop(self):
        if not self.is_empty():
            return self._items.pop()

    def is_empty(self):
        return len(self._items) == 0
```

This class is a fundamental data structure used in many algorithms and problem-solving scenarios. It follows the LIFO (Last In, First Out) principle, meaning that the last item added to the stack is the first one to be removed.

The `__init__` method initializes an empty list, which will be used to store the items in the stack. The `push` method appends an item to the end of the list, effectively adding it to the stack. The `pop` method removes and returns the last item in the list, simulating the behavior of popping an item off the stack. The `is_empty` method checks if the list is empty, indicating whether the stack has any items.

